 tips here make  sure you talk to a doctor or  dermatologist and they can try to work  out what's happening and fix it but if  you follow these tips hopefully it  doesn't get to that point the first tip  for dealing with hand irritation is by  avoiding it in the first place by using  gentle cleansers and sanitizers almost  everyone I talked to for this video  mentioned this my mom's actually been a  nurse for 45 years and this was the main  piece of advice she gave for a while she  had really bad hand  Titus where her skin was cracked and raw  and inflamed even though she was using  tons of hand cream but then the hospital  changed their brand of hand soap and  after a few weeks she didn't actually  need hand cream at all different people  are sensitive to different ingredients  so it might be worth trying a few  different products to see which one  suits you best  my friend who's an anesthetist mentioned  that her Hospital actually supplies a  few options for people to choose from  when you're choosing a hand wash there's  a few things that you can look out for  to improve your chances of finding one  that will work for your skin you've  probably heard a lot about washing your  hands with soap and water but soapy air  actually means any hand cleansing wash  true soaps are alkaline with a high pH  whereas your skin is low pH and acidic  this means that if you're repeatedly  using a true syrup every day it will  disturb your skin's pH and irritated so  free cleansers use synthetic detergents  and these generally end up less  irritating because they can be  formulated at a lower pH they still have  pretty much the same cleansing  properties as a soap they will remove  the virus from your hands and help it  wash down the drain they can also break  down the lipid envelope around the  corona virus and inactivate it so choose  the cleanser with a low pH and make sure  it doesn't contain soap in the  ingredients list if you want to reduce  your chances of irritation in general  products with a shorter ingredients list  will give you a low chance of irritation  than one with a longer list because the  chance of you being sensitive to any of  the ingredients is lower you might learn  that your regular hand wash is actually  true irritating right now because you're  using us so much you can save that for  later and use something that's more  friendly for sensitive skin while you're  washing your hands a lot more some of  the very gentle cleansers that some  dermatologists have recommended include  quv soap free wash aqua nil and dermal  500 a few tips for choosing a gentle  hand sanitizer studies have found that  isopropanol which is isopropyl alcohol  or rubbing alcohol is more irritating on  your skin than ethanol so choose a hand  sanitizer with ethanol if you can  sanitize a second-team moisturizing  ingredients like glycerin have also been  found to reduce irritation and dryness  on your skin different hand sanitizers  also have different ingredients other  than the alcohol and the moisturizing  ingredients so things like fragrances  and press  averages and thickeners so your skin  might tolerate one sanitizer better than  another  just like with home wash choosing a hand  sanitizer with a shorter ingredients  list will probably reduce the chance of  you've reacting to a particular  ingredient in it a tip that you might  not have expected is that hand sanitizer  tends to be less irritating in the long  run than washing your hands if you're in  a position where you have to sanitize  your hands a lot like if you're  healthcare worker using a properly  formulated hand sanitizer with  moisturizing ingredients it's generally  going to be less damaging than  continually washing your hands of course  this depends on the formulas of the  specific hand sanitizers and cleansers  that you're using but generally  sanitizing is better for your hands  because water is actually irritating on  to a few tips for how to wash your hands  don't use hot water for washing her  hands hot water strips your skin of its  natural moisturizing substances much  faster than lukewarm or cold water after  washing Pat your hands thoroughly dry  with a towel rather than rubbing it's  important to get rid of all the water  you can because again water can be  irritating if your hands soak in water  for too long it can break down your skin  and patting rather than rubbing will  reduce irritation wearing gloves and  certain situations can also help it's  also a good idea to avoid getting your  hands wet outside of washing your hands  water is irritating and having your  hands wet for too long can break down  your skin barrier faster so you should  be wearing waterproof gloves when you're  washing your dishes dishwashing  detergent is designed to clean greasy  dishes not dirty hands so it's a lot  harsher than hand soap you also use it  for far longer than the 20 seconds you  need for effective hand-washing  waterproof gloves are also a good idea  when you're preparing food some food can  be irritating for your skin and having  the gloves there to protect your skin  means you won't have to wash your hands  as much when you're wearing gloves make  sure you change them if your hands get  wet inside the glove or too sweaty  because that water can break down your  skin barrier when you're doing housework  or gardening you can wear breathable  fabric gloves to cut down how much you  need to wash your hands it's a good idea  to avoid wearing latex gloves because  latex is a really common allergy if  you're in a place where it's cold and  dry dr. Angela Mata recommends wearing a  pair of winter gloves outside cold air  and exposure to wind can make  and eczema worse using a hand cream is a  pretty obvious solution so here are some  tips on choosing a hand cream there are  a lot of good hand creams out there if  you haven't found a good one yet the  dermatologists I talked to recommended  fragrance free hand creams allergies and  irritati